---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Method">
  <article class="method">
    <h1>Method</h1>

    <p class="method__lead">
      A component library is only reusable if it survives contact with someone else's brand.
      That is the constraint the whole thing is built around.
    </p>

    <h2>Colour is read, not chosen</h2>
    <p>
      Every pixel of a chosen image is converted to OKLab, greys are discarded, and the
      remaining hues are collected into thirty-six bins weighted by chroma. The heaviest bin
      becomes the page hue; the heaviest bin at least sixty degrees away becomes the accent.
    </p>
    <p>
      Lightness is never taken from the image. It sits on a fixed ladder — 98.5 for the page,
      18 for ink, and so on down — which means contrast is a property of the system rather than
      something to measure afterwards. A photograph of a night sky and a photograph of a lemon
      both produce something you can read.
    </p>

    <h2>A literal colour fails the build</h2>
    <p>
      Components reference tokens and nothing else. The registry scans every stylesheet at build
      time, and a hex value, an <code>rgb()</code> or an <code>hsl()</code> stops the build with
      the offending value named. Not a lint warning to be ignored: the site will not compile.
    </p>

    <h2>A missing prompt also fails the build</h2>
    <p>
      Each component carries the prompt that produced it, the attempts that failed first, and
      the reason each failed. A component whose metadata has no final prompt cannot be published
      at all. The code is the easy half — the constraints worth stating up front are the part
      that transfers to the next thing you build.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .method { max-width: var(--measure); padding-top: 6px; }
  .method h1 { margin: 0 0 16px; font-size: clamp(28px, 4vw, 40px); }
  .method__lead { font-family: var(--serif); font-size: 19px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 30px; }
  .method h2 { margin: 32px 0 8px; font-size: 19px; }
  .method p { margin: 0 0 13px; color: var(--text-dim); }
  .method code { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.88em; color: var(--text); }
</style>
